# ============================================================================
#  .gitignore
# ============================================================================
#
#  Excludes build products from version control.
#
#  The repository's tracked content is six lines of C, three documents, a
#  Makefile, seven test scripts, and nine pipeline configuration files.
#  Everything else that appears in the working tree is generated, is
#  derivable from what is tracked, and does not belong in history.
#
#  This matters more here than the size of the artifacts suggests. A
#  committed binary is not merely clutter: it is a 33 KB object whose
#  provenance cannot be verified by reading the repository, it changes on
#  every build for reasons unrelated to the source (see section 5), and
#  once committed it remains in history permanently even after deletion.
#  See section 7 for what to do if that has already happened.
#
#  Coverage of this file is verified on every commit by
#  tests/06-repository.sh, which builds every artifact the Makefile and the
#  workflows are capable of producing and asserts that git ignores each one.
#  A new build product added without a corresponding entry here fails CI.
#
# ============================================================================
#  1. HOW THESE PATTERNS WORK
# ============================================================================
#
#  Four rules govern everything below, and three of them surprise people.
#
#  1.1  A leading slash anchors the pattern to this directory.
#
#       hello       matches a file OR DIRECTORY named "hello" at any depth
#       /hello      matches only ./hello
#
#       The distinction is load-bearing in this repository. The compiled
#       binary is named "hello", and an unanchored pattern would also
#       exclude any directory named hello/ that someone later added --
#       silently, and along with everything inside it. Every entry in
#       section 2 is therefore anchored.
#
#  1.2  A trailing slash restricts the pattern to directories.
#
#       *.dSYM      matches a file or a directory
#       *.dSYM/     matches only a directory
#
#  1.3  The last matching pattern wins.
#
#       Order is significant. A negation placed before the pattern it is
#       meant to override has no effect.
#
#  1.4  A negation cannot rescue a file inside an excluded directory.
#
#       build/
#       !build/keep.txt          <-- does nothing
#
#       Git does not descend into an excluded directory, so it never sees
#       the file the negation refers to. The directory must be re-included
#       first. This is the single most common .gitignore defect and it
#       fails silently.
#
# ============================================================================
#  2. THIS PROJECT'S BUILD PRODUCTS
# ============================================================================
#
#  Enumerated from the Makefile and from .github/workflows/ci.yml rather
#  than guessed at. Each entry names what produces it.

# The program. `make all`, and INSTRUCTIONS.md section 12.1.
/hello

# Windows executable. ci.yml, the `windows` job (MSVC, clang-cl, MinGW).
/hello.exe

# Statically linked build. README section 5.8; ci.yml `benchmark`.
/hello-static

# Sanitizer builds. `make sanitize`; ci.yml `sanitizers`.
/hello-asan
/hello-san

# Alternative and historical toolchains. ci.yml `historical`.
/hello-tcc
/hello-c89
/hello-c++*

# Cross-compiled binaries, one per architecture. ci.yml `cross`.
# The suffix is the matrix key, so the wildcard covers aarch64, armhf,
# riscv64, powerpc64le, and s390x-big-endian without enumerating them.
/hello-aarch64
/hello-armhf
/hello-riscv64
/hello-powerpc64le
/hello-s390x*

# Reproducible-build verification. ci.yml `binary`; tests/04-binary.sh.
/repro
/first.txt
/second.txt

# Compiler diagnostics captured for the zero-diagnostics assertion.
# ci.yml `build`.
/build.log

# Intermediate translation stages. INSTRUCTIONS.md section 12.6 tells the
# reader to generate these by hand, so they turn up in working trees.
/hello.o
/hello.i
/hello.s
*.o
*.i
*.s

# The default output name when -o is omitted. Short for "assembler output",
# a name that dates to the first Unix assembler in 1971 and has outlived
# every assembler that used it.
/a.out

# Source distribution. `make dist`.
/hello-src.tar.gz

# ============================================================================
#  3. TOOLCHAIN AND DEBUGGER ARTIFACTS
# ============================================================================
#
#  Produced by compilers and debuggers rather than by this project's build
#  rules, but produced in this directory nonetheless.

# macOS debug symbol bundles. Created by clang whenever -g is used, which
# `make sanitize` does. A directory, not a file.
*.dSYM/

# Windows debug and build databases.
*.pdb
*.ilk
*.idb
*.obj
*.exp
*.lib

# GCC and Clang coverage and profiling output.
*.gcno
*.gcda
*.gcov
*.profraw
*.profdata

# Static analyser output directories.
/scan-build-*/
/cppcheck-build-dir/

# Core dumps. A program that cannot fail should not produce these; their
# appearance would be genuinely interesting and is still not something to
# commit.
core
core.*
vgcore.*

# ============================================================================
#  4. EDITOR AND OPERATING SYSTEM DETRITUS
# ============================================================================
#
#  These are contentious. The orthodox position is that a personal editor's
#  droppings belong in a global ignore file (~/.config/git/ignore) rather
#  than in a shared repository, because they are a property of the
#  contributor rather than of the project.
#
#  They are listed here anyway, on the practical grounds that
#  INSTRUCTIONS.md Part 6 walks a first-time contributor through choosing
#  an editor, and a repository that invites beginners should not then
#  punish them for using the one it recommended.

# Vim. See INSTRUCTIONS.md section 6.4 if you cannot exit it.
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.netrwhist

# Emacs.
\#*\#
.\#*

# nano. The editor INSTRUCTIONS.md section 6.3 recommends starting with.
*.save
*.save.*

# Visual Studio Code.
/.vscode/

# JetBrains.
/.idea/

# macOS. Created by the Finder merely for having looked at the directory.
.DS_Store
.AppleDouble
._*

# Windows.
Thumbs.db
Desktop.ini

# Linux desktop environments.
.directory
.Trash-*

# ============================================================================
#  5. WHY A BINARY MUST NOT BE COMMITTED
# ============================================================================
#
#  The specific reasons, since "binaries don't belong in git" is usually
#  asserted rather than argued.
#
#  5.1  It is not reproducible from itself. A reader can verify that
#       hello.c prints a greeting by reading six lines. They cannot verify
#       what a committed 33 KB binary does without disassembling it, and
#       Ken Thompson's 1984 Turing Award lecture -- discussed in README
#       section 9.5 -- is the demonstration that this gap is exploitable
#       rather than theoretical.
#
#  5.2  It changes when the source does not. tests/04-binary.sh establishes
#       that this program's compiler output is byte-identical across
#       builds, but only after suppressing the linker's build identifier:
#       Apple's ld64 stamps a fresh LC_UUID into every image, and GNU ld
#       writes a .note.gnu.build-id. A committed binary would therefore
#       produce a diff on every rebuild, attributable to nothing.
#
#  5.3  It is architecture-specific. A binary committed from an x86-64
#       machine is useless on the five architectures in the `cross` matrix,
#       and worse than useless if someone runs it and it silently is not
#       the thing they built.
#
#  5.4  It is permanent. Git stores history, not state. Deleting a
#       committed file in a later commit does not remove it from the
#       repository; it remains in every clone forever. See section 7.
#
#  5.5  The source is smaller. hello.c is 139,770 bytes, of which 139,613
#       are commentary. The compiled binary is 33,432. This is the only
#       repository in which that comparison favours the binary, and it is
#       still the wrong thing to commit.
#
# ============================================================================
#  6. WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT IGNORED
# ============================================================================
#
#  Recorded because an absence is invisible, and because each of these has
#  been excluded by accident in some repository somewhere.
#
#    hello.c             The program. Ignoring the source would be a
#                        remarkable outcome for a pattern of /hello* and is
#                        precisely why section 1.1 anchors and does not
#                        wildcard.
#
#    Makefile            Tracked. `make` is not the build system for the
#                        program -- `cc -o hello hello.c` is -- but it is
#                        the build system for the verification around it,
#                        and it must not drift from what CI runs.
#
#    tests/*.sh          Tracked, including their executable bit. Git
#                        records mode 100755; a suite committed as 100644
#                        fails in CI with "permission denied".
#
#    .github/            Tracked. The workflows are the repository's only
#                        externally-sourced dependency (see
#                        .github/dependabot.yml) and the only part of it
#                        that changes.
#
#    .gitignore          Tracked. A .gitignore that ignores itself is a
#                        joke someone makes once per career; it also means
#                        every contributor rediscovers the exclusions by
#                        committing a binary first.
#
# ============================================================================
#  7. IF A BINARY HAS ALREADY BEEN COMMITTED
# ============================================================================
#
#  This file only affects UNTRACKED files. Adding a pattern here does not
#  untrack anything git is already following, and this is the second most
#  common .gitignore misunderstanding after section 1.4.
#
#  To stop tracking a file while keeping it on disk:
#
#      git rm --cached hello
#      git commit -m "Stop tracking the compiled binary"
#
#  The file remains in the working tree and disappears from future commits.
#  It remains in every previous commit, permanently.
#
#  To verify what is currently tracked that should not be:
#
#      git ls-files | xargs file | grep -i executable
#
#  To check why a given path is or is not ignored, which reports the exact
#  line of this file responsible:
#
#      git check-ignore -v hello
#
#  Removing a file from history entirely requires rewriting every commit
#  that contains it (git filter-repo, or BFG Repo-Cleaner), which changes
#  every subsequent commit hash and requires every collaborator to re-clone.
#  For a 33 KB binary in a repository this size that is disproportionate.
#  For a leaked credential it is mandatory and still insufficient, because
#  the credential must be treated as compromised regardless.
#
# ============================================================================
